{"maxId":74,"entries":{"chapter-44-study-sleep-disorders|Chapter 44 Study: Sleep Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"sleep-amp-aging|Sleep & aging":["u3"],"copy|Sleep cycles roughly every 90 minutes; N3 (slow-wave) sleep declines from early adulthood, more so in men; sleep efficiency falls with age (p643).":["u4"],"copy|Poor sleep tracks with comorbidity burden more than chronological age; excessive daytime sleepiness/napping is linked to amyloid-β accumulation — possible early marker of neurodegeneration (p644).":["u5"],"sleep-disordered-breathing-osa-csa|Sleep-disordered breathing (OSA/CSA)":["u6"],"copy|Adult obstructive sleep apnea can be diagnosed with ≥15 obstructive respiratory events/hour, or ≥5/hour with relevant symptoms or comorbidities. Severe OSA begins at AHI ≥30/hour (p645; ICSD-3-TR, VA/DoD 2025 guideline, Appendix F).":["u7"],"copy|Age-dependent SDB risk factors (Table 44-1, p646): ↑BMI/central obesity, ↓muscle tension, altered airway anatomy, ↑airway collapsibility, ↓thyroid function, ↓lung volume.":["u8"],"copy|Moderate-to-severe SDB estimated in 6% of women, 13% of men in a 2007–2010 cohort of middle-aged adults; OSA underdiagnosed, especially in women (p646).":["u9"],"copy|CSA is two to three times more prevalent at age 65–90 vs 39–64; an estimated upwards of 50% of stable heart-failure patients have some form of SDB (p646).":["u10"],"copy|Neck circumference > 17 inches (men) / > 16 inches (women) predicts SDB, less so in elderly; Friedman palate positions III and IV = high OSA risk (p647).":["u11"],"copy|Peripheral arterial tone–based home devices are not validated for central apneas and should not be used with alpha-blockers or atrial fibrillation; HSAT only for high pretest probability without significant cardiopulmonary comorbidity; a negative HSAT does not rule out OSA (p648–649).":["u12"],"copy|CPAP pressure usually 5 to 20 cm H2O; observational studies found it well tolerated above age 80 and in mild-moderate dementia (p649).":["u13"],"copy|Only 50% to 70% of patients remain CPAP-adherent at 1 year (p649).":["u14"],"copy|SERVE-HF found increased mortality with its ASV device in symptomatic chronic heart failure, LVEF ≤45% and predominantly central sleep apnea. Any consideration of ASV in HFrEF requires an experienced center, shared decision-making and close follow-up (p649; SERVE-HF, AASM 2025 central sleep apnea guideline).":["u15"],"copy|Hypoglossal nerve stimulation trials limited to highly selected CPAP-intolerant patients with BMI less than 32 kg/m2; cohort of 62 patients aged 65–80 showed benefit (p650).":["u16"],"rls-periodic-limb-movement-disorder|RLS / periodic limb movement disorder":["u17"],"copy|RLS affects up to 8% of older adults; 1.5 to 2 times more common in women; two to five times more prevalent in chronic renal failure (p651).":["u18"],"copy|PLMS last 0.5–10 seconds, occur 5–90 seconds apart and comprise at least four consecutive movements. Adult PLMD requires more than 15 movements/hour with clinically significant sleep disturbance or daytime dysfunction unexplained by another disorder (p651; AASM 2025 RLS/PLMD guideline).":["u19"],"copy|PLMS coexist with RLS (80%–90%), RBD (70%), narcolepsy (45%–65%) — PLMD is a diagnosis of exclusion (p651).":["u20"],"copy|PLMS seen on PSG in up to 45% of older adults, often not clinically significant (p652).":["u21"],"copy|Check ferritin and transferrin saturation in clinically significant RLS. AASM guidance considers iron at ferritin ≤75 µg/L or transferrin saturation <20%, with IV iron when ferritin is 75–100 µg/L if iron is indicated. The moderate–severe RLS trial permitting ferritin 75–300 µg/L required transferrin saturation <20% in that range (p652; trial, AASM 2025 RLS/PLMD guideline).":["u22"],"copy|Dopamine-agonist augmentation risk 2% to 15% per year, worse with higher dose/duration (p652).":["u23"],"copy|RLS drug selection. The AASM favors gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil and pregabalin when appropriate. FDA-labeled gabapentin enacarbil is 600 mg daily for RLS with normal renal function; 1,200 mg adds adverse effects without additional benefit. Dopamine agonists can cause augmentation and behavioral disinhibition. If pramipexole is chosen, the labeled RLS titration is 0.125–0.5 mg once daily. The labeled ropinirole range begins at 0.25 mg, up to 4 mg daily. Table 44-2 lists carbidopa/levodopa 25/100–25/200 mg for infrequent use because of augmentation. Table 44-2 lists off-label gabapentin 100–1,800 mg and pregabalin 300 mg as studied doses. Adjust for renal function and monitor sedation. The table lists clonazepam 0.25–2 mg and temazepam 15–30 mg, with limited evidence and cognitive/fall risks. Its off-label opioid examples are codeine 30–60 mg, tramadol 50–100 mg, oxycodone 5–15 mg, hydrocodone 5–15 mg and methadone 5–10 mg. These are historical table ranges. Specialist assessment of respiratory, dependence and medication risks is essential in refractory RLS (p653; FDA prescribing information for Horizant and pramipexole, AASM 2025 RLS/PLMD guideline).Current practice (not the book): FDA-labelled RLS dosing of pramipexole is 0.125 mg once daily 2–3 h before bedtime, titrated if needed to 0.25 then 0.5 mg; no evidence that 0.75 mg adds benefit — US prescribing information (pramipexole tablets, DailyMed), 2026; Current practice (not the book): AASM conditionally suggests against standard pramipexole, transdermal rotigotine or ropinirole use in adults with RLS because of augmentation with longer-term treatment; evidence certainty is moderate for pramipexole/ropinirole and low for rotigotine; patients prioritizing short-term symptom relief over long-term adverse effects may still choose these agents - AASM 2025, recommendations 12-14, p144":["u24"],"rem-sleep-behavior-disorder-rbd|REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD)":["u25"],"copy|Prevalence ~1% over age 40, ~2% over age 60; male-predominant, typically presents mid-60s (p654).":["u26"],"copy|Diagnosis requires REM sleep without atonia plus dream-enactment behavior (p654).":["u27"],"copy|Up to 90% of RBD patients develop a synucleinopathy (Parkinson, Lewy body dementia, MSA) over 10- to 15-year follow-up (p654).":["u28"],"copy|Melatonin is the primary first choice in most circumstances; avoiding benzodiazepines is generally preferable in older patients. In a single-center retrospective study, only melatonin was associated with reduced injuries/falls and had a better side-effect profile than clonazepam (p655).":["u29"],"circadian-rhythm-disorders|Circadian rhythm disorders":["u30"],"copy|Advanced sleep-wake phase disorder affects ~1% of a middle-aged population; more common with aging (p657).":["u31"],"copy|Irregular sleep-wake rhythm common in Alzheimer, Parkinson, and Huntington disease; sundowning may be a phenotype of a circadian rhythm disturbance (p657).":["u32"],"copy|Tasimelteon is an MT1/MT2 agonist approved for Non-24-hour sleep-wake disorder in adults and for nighttime sleep disturbances in Smith–Magenis syndrome. The formulation for the latter depends on age (p657; FDA Hetlioz prescribing information).":["u33"],"insomnia|Insomnia":["u34"],"copy|Chronic insomnia causes sleep difficulty despite adequate opportunity, with daytime consequences, at least 3 nights/week for at least 3 months. Short-term insomnia lasts less than 3 months (p657; ICSD-3-TR, VA/DoD 2025 guideline, Appendix F).":["u35"],"copy|Prevalence up to 10% in young adults, rising to 30% to 48% after age 65; more common in women; up to 40% of caregivers use sleep aids (p658).":["u36"],"copy|In one study, a third of older patients had persistent severe insomnia at 4-year follow-up; more than 80% of women over 85 report sleeping difficulties (p658).":["u37"],"copy|CBT-I is first-line treatment for all adults with insomnia (p659).":["u38"],"copy|Stimulus control: get out of bed if not asleep within 20 minutes (p662).":["u39"],"copy|Sleep restriction: time-in-bed = average sleep time + 15 minutes, advance 15 min once efficiency 80%–85%, goal ~8 hours (p662).":["u40"],"copy|Drugs: Benzodiazepines ↓ sleep latency ~10 minutes, ↑ total sleep time 30 to 60 minutes; avoid long-acting flurazepam/quazepam; all Beers-listed (p659). Temazepam start 7.5 mg, usual 7.5–30 mg, half-life 8.8 h — caution: psychomotor impairment, increased fall risk; eszopiclone start 1 mg, usual 1–2 mg, half-life 6 h — caution: avoid high-fat meals, unpleasant taste/headache (Table 44-5, p660). Zolpidem/zaleplon — rapid onset, take immediately before bed; Beers-listed (p659). Ramelteon (MT1/MT2 agonist) — few side effects; lack of amnestic properties makes it somewhat less effective for subjective improvement in sleep (p660). Suvorexant and lemborexant are dual orexin receptor antagonists for insomnia. The suvorexant trial in mild–moderate probable Alzheimer dementia randomized 285, of whom 277 completed. Placebo-adjusted total sleep time improved by 28 minutes at week 4 (p660; Herring et al., FDA Belsomra prescribing information). Doxepin low-dose 3–6 mg for insomnia vs 100–300 mg as an antidepressant (p661). Avoid diphenhydramine as primary therapy — long half-life, next-day sedation, rapid tolerance, dry mouth, urinary retention, delirium, decreased cognition (p661).":["u41"],"institutionalized-dementia|Institutionalized / dementia":["u42"],"copy|Nocturnal wandering and confusion are a leading cause of institutionalization; CPAP can be used successfully in mild-moderate dementia, and treating SDB can improve cognitive function (p662–663).":["u43"],"recall|Recall":["u44"],"copy|CHAPTER 44s / HAZZARD":["u45"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 44 — Sleep Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u46"],"copy|1.":["u47"],"copy|What AHI value alone is diagnostic of SDB, and what value defines severe disease?":["u48"],"copy|At least 15 obstructive respiratory events/hour suffice, or at least 5/hour with relevant symptoms or comorbidities.":["u49"],"copy|An AHI of 30/hour or more is severe (p645; ICSD-3-TR).":["u49"],"copy|3.":["u50"],"copy|What neck circumference cutoffs raise suspicion for SDB in men and women?":["u51"],"copy|Greater than 17 inches in men, greater than 16 inches in women (predictive value is weaker in older patients) (p647).":["u52"],"copy|5.":["u53"],"copy|Below what ejection fraction is adaptive servo-ventilation (ASV) generally not recommended for central sleep apnea, and why?":["u54"],"copy|LVEF ≤45% in symptomatic chronic heart failure with predominantly central sleep apnea.":["u55"],"copy|SERVE-HF found higher mortality with its ASV device in this population (p649; PMID 26323938).":["u55"],"copy|8.":["u56"],"copy|What is \"augmentation\" in RLS pharmacotherapy, and how often does it occur per year on long-acting dopamine agonists?":["u57"],"copy|Worsening of RLS — earlier onset, increased intensity, and/or spread to arms/trunk; estimated at 2% to 15% per year, more likely with higher dose and longer duration (p652).":["u58"],"copy|10.":["u59"],"copy|What proportion of REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) patients go on to develop a synucleinopathy, and over what time frame?":["u60"],"copy|Up to 90%, over 10 to 15 years of follow-up (p654).":["u61"],"copy|11.":["u62"],"copy|What is the preferred first-choice medication for RBD in older adults, and why?":["u63"],"copy|Melatonin — in a single-center retrospective study, associated with fewer injuries/falls and a better side-effect profile than clonazepam; avoiding benzodiazepines is generally preferable in older patients (p655).":["u64"],"copy|15.":["u65"],"copy|Which two benzodiazepines should be avoided in older adults with insomnia because of next-day effects?":["u66"],"copy|Flurazepam and quazepam (long-acting agents) (p659).":["u67"],"copy|Answer":["u68","u69","u70","u71","u72","u73","u74"],"copy|In selected longitudinal cohorts, up to 90% of people presenting with isolated RBD developed a clinically evident synucleinopathy (Parkinson, Lewy body dementia, MSA) over 10- to 15-year follow-up (p654).":["u28"],"copy|Which heart-failure population had excess mortality with the ASV device in SERVE-HF, and how does this inform referral?":["u54"],"copy|SERVE-HF found higher mortality with its ASV device in this population.":["u55"],"copy|The AASM limits consideration of ASV for central apnea in HFrEF to experienced centers with shared decision-making and close follow-up (p649).":["u55"],"copy|AASM 2025, recommendation 4":["u55"],"copy|What long-term risk of a clinically evident synucleinopathy has been described in selected cohorts presenting with isolated RBD?":["u60"],"copy|Up to 90% over 10 to 15 years in selected clinical cohorts.":["u61"],"copy|This estimate applies to isolated RBD and is not an individual prediction (p654, Figure 44-8).":["u61"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[94,111],[95,-1],[206,0]]}
